WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.900
A familiar tale of a wooden boy is&nbsp;
seen in a whole new light through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.376
the visionary imagination of one of&nbsp;
the greatest living horror directors.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.780
2022 has seen a bevy of amazing stop-motion&nbsp;
animated movies. And closing off this year of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.920
stop-motion madness is possibly the most&nbsp;
exciting project of them all as Academy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:22.380
Award winner Guillermo del Toro puts his own&nbsp;
spin on the classic 1883 Carlo Collodi novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.400
"Pinocchio." The well-known story has seen&nbsp;
multiple adaptations throughout the decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.140
most notably Walt Disney's beloved 1940&nbsp;
version. Still, del Toro seems to be offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.340
a completely new vision of this classic tale&nbsp;
that will surely take audiences by surprise.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:40.267
"I am Guillermo del Toro. This is my Pinocchio."

00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:43.920
Guillermo del Toro is best known for&nbsp;
his lush array of genre films such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.100
as "Pan's Labyrinth," "Crimson Peak,"&nbsp;
"Hellboy" and the best-picture-winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.940
"The Shape of Water." He has had his eyes&nbsp;
on the famous wooden character, Pinocchio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.320
for some time. Originally announced back&nbsp;
in 2008, del Toro has not only been in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.340
love with the "Pinocchio" story itself but has&nbsp;
always envisioned it as an animated feature.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.660
And now, "Guillermo del Toro's Pinocchio" is&nbsp;
set for release in select theaters throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:09.540
November 2022 but will be available to stream on&nbsp;
Netflix on December 9, 2022. Its world premiere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:14.580
came on October 15, 2022, at the BFI London&nbsp;
Film Festival, which will be followed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:20.400
Animation is Film festival on the 29th and an&nbsp;
official US Premiere at AFI Fest on November 5.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.360
The film has seen a troubled production over&nbsp;
the past few years, as multiple release dates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:29.100
have been set and reworked. Alongside fellow&nbsp;
upcoming stop motion Netflix film "Wendell and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.180
Wild," "Guillermo del Toro's Pinocchio" was&nbsp;
initially slated for a release sometime in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.440
2021. The film, along with several other&nbsp;
high-profile Netflix animated projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.640
were moved to 2022, with Netflix&nbsp;
CEO Ted Sarandos revealing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.640
the company has plans to release&nbsp;
up to six animated features a year.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.380
But that's nothing compared to the film's earlier&nbsp;
troubles. Originally intended to be released in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:54.960
2013 or 2014, the film went through several years&nbsp;
of development hell as no studio was willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.920
put up the finances to produce it. Things got&nbsp;
so out of hand that the director canceled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.240
ambitious project, even after getting "Over&nbsp;
the Garden Wall" creator Patrick McHale as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.530
screenwriter. Thankfully in 2018, Netflix&nbsp;
came to the rescue and greenlit the film.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.220
The plot of "Guillermo del Toro's&nbsp;
Pinocchio," while following the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.940
general outline of the classic story, seems&nbsp;
to have more in store in its setting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.900
presentation. The description of the film&nbsp;
given by a tweet from Netflix Film reads,

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:24.240
"Set during the rise of Fascism in Mussolini's&nbsp;
Italy, PINOCCHIO ... is a story of love and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.840
disobedience as Pinocchio struggles to&nbsp;
live up to his father's expectations."

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.220
This setting, deviating from the fairytale&nbsp;
world audiences are accustomed to, is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.780
line with Del Toro's dark, fantastical vision,&nbsp;
staying more faithful to the original Carlo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:41.274
Collodi tale "The Adventures of Pinocchio,"&nbsp;
which the director describes as having a

00:02:41.274 --> 00:02:45.720
" ... far more perverse and spooky and&nbsp;
semi-necrophilia vibe to it in certain aspects."

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.500
One element it will share with the Disney&nbsp;
classic is that del Toro's film is set to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.060
be a musical. Writing both the musical numbers&nbsp;
and scoring the film is Alexandre Desplat. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.080
will be Desplat's second time working with&nbsp;
del Toro after winning an Academy Award for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:03.060
his work on "The Shape of Water" in 2017. It&nbsp;
will also be Desplat's third stop motion film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:07.020
having worked with director Wes Anderson and&nbsp;
being nominated for Academy Awards for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:12.449
musical scores of both 2009's "Fantastic&nbsp;
Mr. Fox" and 2018's "Isle of Dogs."

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.680
"Guillermo del Toro's Pinocchio" will&nbsp;
see a star-studded cast take on these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.520
classic characters. But voicing the&nbsp;
titular role is newcomer Gregory Mann.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.460
"Oh, what a day, what a day."

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:27.480
Mann's time working on the project was&nbsp;
a positive one. At the film's BFI London&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.320
Film Festival premiere he talked about&nbsp;
the director to "HeyUGuys" saying quote,

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:35.580
"He's incredible. I mean, he's just as good as&nbsp;
he sounds. He's amazing to work with as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:39.480
he's so kind, and he brings such a nice&nbsp;
spirit to the recording studio or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:43.140
set. And he really made me look at lines&nbsp;
and look at them into deeper context."

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:48.540
Playing as Pinocchio's closest allies, the Cricket&nbsp;
and Geppetto are Ewan McGregor and David Bradley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:53.820
respectively. Alongside them are Christoph Waltz&nbsp;
as the villainous Count Volpe, Cate Blanchett as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:59.220
the monkey puppeteer Spazzatura, Ron Perlman as&nbsp;
The Podestà, Finn Wolfhard as The Podestà's son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:02.820
Candlewick, and Tilda Swinton as the&nbsp;
Wood Sprite who gives Pinocchio life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:08.126
Tim Blake Nelson, John Turturro, and Burn&nbsp;
Gorman round out the cast in unknown roles.

00:04:08.126 --> 00:04:12.660
Netflix has currently released two trailers for&nbsp;
"Guillermo del Toro's Pinocchio." The first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:17.400
released in January 2022, acts as a brief&nbsp;
teaser where Ewan McGregor's Cricket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.380
or Sebastian J. Cricket as he calls himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.602
prepares to write something while&nbsp;
telling the audience an interesting fact.

00:04:22.602 --> 00:04:28.183
"I lived, actually lived, in&nbsp;
the heart of the wooden boy."

00:04:28.183 --> 00:04:32.040
Whether this is meant to be taken literally&nbsp;
or this version of the Cricket will stand as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:36.420
a representation of Pinocchio's heart rather&nbsp;
than his conscience is yet to be seen. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:40.080
the teaser excited viewers for what was to&nbsp;
come, just from the beautiful look alone.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.660
A full film trailer was released in late July&nbsp;
2022. In it, we see Geppetto, who appears to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:49.500
have lost a child of his own, build and piece&nbsp;
together Pinocchio. From there, we're treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:54.540
to a true feast for the eyes as Pinocchio goes&nbsp;
about the town, meets both good and evil faces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:59.220
and warmly interacts with Geppetto. It's hard not&nbsp;
to get swept up in the overwhelming splendor of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:03.420
the animation on display. From the beautifully&nbsp;
textured and designed characters to the grand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:08.220
sets to the outstanding visual effects, this&nbsp;
trailer alone proves why the dazzling art form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.900
of stop-motion animation will never truly die&nbsp;
out. Top it off with a sweeping musical score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:16.200
and you can count us in for whatever&nbsp;
Guillermo del Toro has in store.

